I've been chucking this idea out for a while, and a recent thread
finally inspired me to flesh out the details. I think there should be
an option in nethack to reject your own Gods and serve Moloch. It would
make the game a little more open-ended, and it also fits logically into
a (imho) much-needed shakeup of the alignment system.

Here is one set of changes that would introduce this:

- Re-arrange the alignment system so that (a) you can't raise alignment
to max simply by slaughtering a load of arbitrary monsters and (b) it
relies instead on more 'in-character' means of raising/ lowering it:
for example using magic improves wizardliness for wizards, but reduces
barbarian-ness for barbarians. Things like eating meat as a monk,
killing as a healer, reading as a caveperson, breaking statues as an
arch would become (cumulatively) significant for your alignment over
the course of the game. Killing your quest leader and/or cronies would
be a massive no-no, obviously. The 'divine animals' YANI suggested
elsethread might work here as well. [1]

-Change it so that killing the quest leader gives you access to the
quest levels.

-If your alignment gets really really low, your own God may try to kill
you by sending in monsters. Killing them just pisses the God off more,
so you should run from them and quickly try to appease the deity on
another level. Your quest leader should also require a decent alignment
level, and may even turn hostile if it's too low.

-Successful completion of the book/bell/candle ritual requires that you
be in good favour with your current deity.

-When you first meet the quest nemesis (however you got there), he will
give you the choice of joining him rather than fighting. He will make
this offer only once. If your alignment is low and you accept, you will
be converted to a servant of Moloch. If your alignment is too high and
you accept, Moloch rejects you, and your alignment to your existing
deity takes the kind of battering from which it it very difficult
indeed to recover.

- You can also try to convert to Moloch by praying at an appropriate
altar. Obviously, this will annoy the regular pantheon as well. Note
that after your first failed conversion attempt, Moloch will reject you
permanently. This is so you can't stroll up to an altar of Moloch with
high alignment, unsuccessfully attempt conversion once to lower
alignment, then convert successfully on the second try.

- Once you become a servant of Moloch, there is no possible return to
the normal pantheon. (Although a helm of opposite alignment should have
interesting effects). Moloch is a difficult deity to serve: He doesn't
hand out many favours, and since he is 'unaligned', all items
(including water) should work as uncursed, regardless of actual status.
You can also expect to be hounded at all times by &s, Es and As sent at
regular intervals by the normal pantheon to kill you. On the plus side,
you can pray to Moloch in gehenommonemenon.

-If you approach the nemesis as a servant of Moloch or convert
successfully when he offers, he becomes peaceful and tells you of your
new objective: Apparently Moloch has become distrustful of Rodney and
the high priest, who have grown ambitious and secretive. Moloch seeks a
worthy replacement to guard the amulet for him. The nemesis was going
to go for it himself, but [insert reason why not here]. The nemesis
then gives you the bell of opening, but keeps the quest artifact. You
can fight him for it if you like, but this will not make Moloch at all
happy (equivalent to killing quest leader)

-The objective for followers of Moloch is to kill rodney, kill the high
priest and pick up the amulet:
As you strike your final blow, the high priest collapses to the ground
--more--
You pick up the Amulet --more--
The wizard of Yendor appears. "Finally, I have caught up with you,
usurper. Prepare to be destroyed!"
The dread voice of Moloch fills the sanctum --more--
"No, Rodney, for too long you have plotted against me. It is you who
shall be destroyed."
A steaming pit opens in the ground, and vile things reach out to drag
the Wizard of Yendor in to his ultimate doom. --more--
Moloch intones:
"You are my most worthy and trusted servant. I bestow upon thee the
gift of immortality. --more--
 Remain in this place and guard my precious amulet against all who
covet it."
And so on.
DYWYPI?

While following Moloch does have it's own challenges (As, Es, &s, no
blessed or cursed items), it could be argued that effectively skipping
the planes makes this "descension" somewhat easier than an
old-fashioned ascension. Also, immortality under Moloch is somewhat
less than certain. (How long before he stops trusting  *you*?)  so a
descension would probably still be regarded as a 'second best' win, but
it would be fun nonetheless.

